0b7 Wed \u00b7 Fri Free to read [aria-label=Illustration] [alt=A hand-sewn felt robot lies on an operating table under a surgical lamp. Its chest seam is unpicked and the stuffing shows.] under.the.hood Real, or theatre? the stuffing Real the visor Theatre [aria-label=AI, unpicked.] AI, unpicked. What's coming in AI, before it's obvious. Three times a week I take one AI story apart: who gets paid, what's real, and where it leads. You make your mind up. [aria-label=Sections] follow.the.money Mon Mondays under.the.hood Wed Wednesdays mind.the.gap Fri Fridays Get every piece in your inbox [placeholder=you@work.com] Subscribe free follow.the.money \u00b7 Mondays No added sermons under.the.hood \u00b7 Wednesdays Free from jargon mind.the.gap \u00b7 Fridays Every call scored in public Contains British spelling follow.the.money \u00b7 Mondays No added sermons under.the.hood \u00b7 Wednesdays Free from jargon mind.the.gap \u00b7 Fridays Every call scored in public Contains British spelling On the newsstand Fresh off the press. 14 October 2026 Meta\u2019s Muse AI, reviewed. Amazon already locked it out, and Walmart let it right in. There are now three kinds of AI. Meta's Muse is the new kind, its real rival is Siri, and the shops are already picking sides. Read it \u2197 12 October 2026 Warner sued Suno, and now it\u2019s taking a cut. Who else is going to get paid? Warner, BMG and Believe now get a share of Suno's revenue. Universal and Sony sue it again. Here is who gets paid when a fan makes a song with AI. Read it \u2197 7 October 2026 Higgsfield, taken apart: the $1bn AI video machine What's real, what's theatre, and what it bodes for you choosing your AI models. Read it \u2197 Contents What's inside, every week. Illustrations. The real ones name every source, show the evidence for every stamp and come with dates. follow.the.money Mondays p. 04 Bring a calculator Who gets paid when AI gets bought. [aria-label=Money flows from you, the buyer to the app, then on to the cloud and the model lab. your old supplier: money that stopped coming.] you, the buyer the app the cloud the model lab your old supplier money that stopped coming Bring a calculator The question every piece answers Where does the money move, and who ends up better or worse off? Every AI deal has a bill, and somebody pays it. Every Monday we trace the money from the invoice to the bank account, and show who comes out ahead and who gets stuck with the tab. under.the.hood Wednesdays p. 08 Screwdriver included Real, or theatre? We take it apart. [aria-label=the shiny new thing, taken apart: the model is Real, the launch video is Theatre, the data is Real, the waitlist is Theatre, the people checking it is Real.] the shiny new thing the model REAL the launch video THEATRE the data REAL the waitlist THEATRE the people checking it REAL Screwdriver included The question every piece answers What actually goes together in a shipped thing, and why did this one work? Every Wednesday we open up something that shipped and actually works. Every part gets a stamp, real or theatre, so you can see what does the work and what is there for the launch video. mind.the.gap Fridays p. 12 The headliner What's coming, before it's obvious. [aria-label=the price, the chips, the people meet at the pattern, then our call, dated] THEN NOW NEXT the price the chips the people the pattern our call, dated The headliner The question every piece answers What is the pattern, and what does it mean is coming? Most weeks, a pile of AI stories look like they have nothing to do with each other. Every Friday we lay the threads side by side, show where they cross, and put a date on what we think happens next. Illustrations. The real ones name every source, show the evidence for every stamp and come with dates. The back page Every piece makes a call. We keep score. Each piece ends with a prediction and the date it is due. When the date arrives, we mark it right, wrong or too close to call. The wrong ones go up as big as the right ones. Misses included Example board Example board. Right: 14, Wrong: 5, Too close to call: 2, Not due yet: 9 1 4 Right 0 5 Wrong 0 2 Too close to call 0 9 Not due yet A major airline lets an AI agent rebook a missed flight with no human in the loop. Due 30 June 2026 Right A top-ten bank lets you open an account by talking to an AI agent alone. Due 31 August 2026 Wrong By 30 June 2027, Higgsfield raises money at a valuation of $10 billion or more. Due 30 June 2027 Not due yet An example of how the board reads. The full scoreboard opens once there are enough calls to count. Also available in Pick your screen. Live Substack The home of every piece. Read it in full, free, in your inbox or on the web. Read on Substack \u2197 Live YouTube One Short per piece. The whole argument, fast. Watch now \u2197 Live Instagram The Short, plus a flipbook carousel you swipe through. Follow \u2197 Live TikTok The same Shorts, on your For You page. Watch now \u2197 Live X Krish on what just moved in AI, as it moves. Follow \u2197 [alt=Krish Raja, editor of makeyourmindup, pushing his glasses up for a closer look] Krish Raja [alt=Krish Raja, editor of makeyourmindup, pushing his glasses up for a closer look] The staff box Who makes this. Editor Krish Raja Written for Busy people who want AI explained without the sales pitch Checked by A blind panel of AI judges , then Krish, who gets the final say Jargon Translated on arrival Sermons None. A machine checks. Em dashes Also none Krish writes every piece, reads the audio edition and fronts the Shorts. He has spent sixteen years building commercial businesses across media, tech and telecoms, and now spends most days building with AI. Meet the judges \u2197 krishraja.com \u2197 LinkedIn \u2197 The panel [aria-label=Close] \u2715 Ten judges read every draft blind: no byline, no author, no idea what Krish liked last time. Any of them can stop a piece. None of them can approve one. Tap a face to meet one. [aria-label=The Preach